WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.252 --> 00:00:06.172
The story at the heart of
Gucci is extraordinary.

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:07.966
There are a lot of rumors around.

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:12.012
It has got passions,

00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:13.555
it's got family, it's got feuding.

00:00:13.555 --> 00:00:15.974
It's got loves and loathing and tragedy,

00:00:15.974 --> 00:00:17.976
even murder.

00:00:17.976 --> 00:00:20.937
It's better to cry in Rolls-Royce

00:00:20.937 --> 00:00:23.148
than to be happy on a bicycle.

00:00:24.065 --> 00:00:28.194
That story is really the
appeal and the excitement

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:32.699
and yes, the sex factor
of a brand like Gucci.

00:00:34.659 --> 00:00:37.662
Sometimes things happen in
life that change your life.

00:00:37.662 --> 00:00:41.708
This was really success
beyond my wildest imagination.

00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:43.460
I didn't believe possible,

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.588
Tom did not believe it was possible.

00:00:46.588 --> 00:00:48.131
The success was unbelievable.

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:52.052
It is difficult to understate

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:54.888
just how successful the Gucci brand

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:55.889
has been in history,

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:57.849
but also really is right now.

00:00:57.849 --> 00:01:01.186
We're looking at a brand value
of something like $15 billion

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:04.773
and that's underpinned by an extraordinary

00:01:04.773 --> 00:01:06.149
social media following.

00:01:06.149 --> 00:01:08.068
So you both got this business value

00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:11.654
and also you've got this
dynamism in the digital world.

00:01:11.654 --> 00:01:15.075
That combination has really been the key

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:17.202
to Gucci's success.

00:01:18.953 --> 00:01:21.289
There's been an unfairness

00:01:21.289 --> 00:01:24.501
to give a lot of credit to people

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:27.003
who really didn't deserve all that credit.

00:01:27.003 --> 00:01:29.422
And instead people who had deserved credit

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:31.132
were totally forgotten.

00:01:31.132 --> 00:01:33.510
Nobody has focused on the real story.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.513
Everybody has focused on the fairy tale.

00:01:36.513 --> 00:01:38.807
That's very good news for the viewers

00:01:38.807 --> 00:01:40.308
and maybe also for the media

00:01:40.308 --> 00:01:41.559
but it's not the reality.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:57.700
There is an epic saying
in Italy when it comes to

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:02.455
family generations that the
first generation creates,

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:04.624
the second generation expands,

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:07.127
and the third generation destroys.

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:08.920
And that's almost what happened

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.547
in the Gucci story.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:15.218
You've got the founder
who was Guccio Gucci

00:02:15.218 --> 00:02:19.055
who had the vision for
making quality leather goods

00:02:19.055 --> 00:02:21.641
and selling them initially
to American tourists

00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:24.269
and other European travelers.

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:28.356
And then you have his two
sons, Rodolfo and Aldo,

00:02:28.356 --> 00:02:32.026
who ended up having
each 50% of the company.

00:02:32.026 --> 00:02:36.531
And especially Aldo
was really the marketer

00:02:36.531 --> 00:02:37.991
and the expander,

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:41.870
and he had a vision to take
Gucci outside of Italy.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:43.663
And then you have the third generation

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:45.874
which is also where a lot
of the conflicts started

00:02:45.874 --> 00:02:48.585
because you had Aldo's three sons,

00:02:48.585 --> 00:02:50.670
Roberto, Georgio, and Paolo,

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.631
and they had 50% together
with their father.

00:02:53.631 --> 00:02:56.050
And then you have Maurizio
on the other side,

00:02:56.050 --> 00:03:00.597
a single child who inherited
50% from his father Rodolfo.

00:03:08.646 --> 00:03:10.982
The Gucci story was really punctuated

00:03:10.982 --> 00:03:12.859
by the family conflicts

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:15.612
and they were extremely litigious.

00:03:15.612 --> 00:03:19.073
So, all of these fights
spilled out into open court

00:03:19.073 --> 00:03:20.742
and became extremely public.

00:03:23.453 --> 00:03:26.748
One of the characters that
really had the longest arc

00:03:26.748 --> 00:03:30.126
and almost the most
knowledge about the story

00:03:30.126 --> 00:03:32.170
was Domenico De Sole

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:36.674
who started as a lawyer to
Rodolfo in the early '80s.

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:39.427
So, hi, Domenico.

00:03:39.427 --> 00:03:40.261
Hi. How are you?

00:03:40.261 --> 00:03:42.472
So what was it like working for Rodolfo

00:03:42.472 --> 00:03:44.140
and then dealing with Aldo

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.351
and the family?
It was very complicated

00:03:46.351 --> 00:03:49.562
because that point what was happening

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:51.856
there was a big conflict in the family

00:03:51.856 --> 00:03:55.026
because Aldo's children probably felt

00:03:55.026 --> 00:03:57.362
that their dad had done
such a phenomenal job,

00:03:57.362 --> 00:03:58.196
which is true.

00:03:58.196 --> 00:04:00.281
Aldo was a pretty formidable person

00:04:00.281 --> 00:04:04.410
but they felt that they deserve
more than the 1/4 of 50%.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:07.121
So we had a lot of battle
that went on and on

00:04:07.121 --> 00:04:08.623
for a long time.

00:04:08.623 --> 00:04:11.459
But then the situation
was basically confirmed

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:14.837
and there was no change in
the shareholder ownership,

00:04:14.837 --> 00:04:17.674
and Maurizio kept the 50%.

00:04:17.674 --> 00:04:18.883
And that was the situation

00:04:18.883 --> 00:04:20.510
but that created a lot of animosity,

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:22.262
there where a lot of battle.

00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:24.889
He dreams every five minutes.

00:04:24.889 --> 00:04:26.808
He doesn't think, he dreams.

00:04:26.808 --> 00:04:31.688
And in his own life, he'd
still run as a child.

00:04:31.688 --> 00:04:35.692
Today, we do $138 million of business

00:04:35.692 --> 00:04:38.027
with $18 million profit.

00:04:39.529 --> 00:04:41.114
This is the answer to a dream.

00:04:42.156 --> 00:04:42.991
I accept.

00:04:44.742 --> 00:04:48.371
So major turning point
in this family story

00:04:48.371 --> 00:04:53.376
was 1983 Rodolfo is very ill and he dies,

00:04:53.668 --> 00:04:56.504
and he leaves his 50% stake in the company

00:04:56.504 --> 00:04:58.089
to his only son Maurizio.

00:04:59.132 --> 00:05:02.260
Maurizio is starting to get
his ideas even back then

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:05.346
about how he wants to
clean up the Gucci brand

00:05:05.346 --> 00:05:06.764
and take it up market,

00:05:06.764 --> 00:05:09.892
but he can't get his uncle
and his cousins on board.

00:05:09.892 --> 00:05:13.062
They're very happy with all of
the lucrative licensing deals

00:05:13.062 --> 00:05:16.357
that are very profitable for them.

00:05:16.357 --> 00:05:20.153
So as Maurizio is becoming
more and more convinced

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:22.155
of his vision for Gucci,

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:24.991
and that he's the one who
needs to take it forward,

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:27.869
on the home front he's also
falling out with his wife

00:05:27.869 --> 00:05:30.163
Patrizia who had been pushing him

00:05:30.163 --> 00:05:32.749
to be the kind of Mr. Gucci

00:05:32.749 --> 00:05:36.210
and one of the leading
luxury CEOs in Milan.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.921
But he's starting to
feel a little bit pressed

00:05:38.921 --> 00:05:43.134
and in 1985, he decides to leave her.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.137
We have this transformation.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.307
Mr. Jekyll became Dr. Hyde.

00:05:55.229 --> 00:05:59.067
The same time you had Paolo
ratting on his father, Aldo,

00:05:59.067 --> 00:06:00.818
to the US tax authorities

00:06:00.818 --> 00:06:04.781
because he suspected his
father of siphoning off profits

00:06:04.781 --> 00:06:06.866
and putting them into offshore accounts.

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:10.244
So, there was a case
then started by the IRS,

00:06:10.244 --> 00:06:14.248
an investigation that ended
up sending Aldo to jail.

00:06:14.248 --> 00:06:15.249
I never forget.

00:06:15.249 --> 00:06:17.794
I remember we had a board
meeting of Gucci America

00:06:17.794 --> 00:06:19.295
and Maurizio asked me, and said,

00:06:19.295 --> 00:06:21.798
"You have to explain what this means."

00:06:21.798 --> 00:06:24.092
And yes, I tried to explain
to the members of family,

00:06:24.092 --> 00:06:25.676
this is the United
States, it's very serious.

00:06:25.676 --> 00:06:28.221
On tax fraud is very
serious, it's not Italy.

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:30.723
One member of the board said to me,

00:06:30.723 --> 00:06:32.100
"You are pessimistic."

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:32.975
I said, "Oh, maybe.

00:06:32.975 --> 00:06:34.477
I don't know what you want me to say."

00:06:34.477 --> 00:06:35.812
Unfortunately, Aldo did go to jail

00:06:35.812 --> 00:06:38.022
and at that point,

00:06:38.022 --> 00:06:39.690
again, it's a big change in my life

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.943
because I went from being the lawyer

00:06:41.943 --> 00:06:44.153
for the company and the family.

00:06:44.153 --> 00:06:46.614
He asked me to run Gucci America

00:06:46.614 --> 00:06:50.493
and with a specific goal
of settling with the IRS.

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:57.417
The reputation was still
at a very good reputation.

00:06:57.417 --> 00:07:00.044
Most importantly
worldwide name recognition

00:07:00.044 --> 00:07:02.171
because Aldo, they're
awfully really geniuses.

00:07:02.171 --> 00:07:05.967
They open store in places like Japan

00:07:05.967 --> 00:07:07.260
in Hong Kong, in Asia,

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.512
you know the really amazing early

00:07:09.512 --> 00:07:11.681
on they were in all these markets.

00:07:11.681 --> 00:07:13.099
But there was a problem,

00:07:13.099 --> 00:07:14.892
I think the product was really getting

00:07:14.892 --> 00:07:16.811
to be less attractive.

00:07:16.811 --> 00:07:20.064
They also expanded
distribution dramatically.

00:07:20.064 --> 00:07:22.900
So there were a lot of events that occur

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:26.529
that really the brand was deteriorating.

00:07:32.326 --> 00:07:35.079
There are many lessons
from luxury brand history

00:07:35.079 --> 00:07:37.081
about the balance between

00:07:37.081 --> 00:07:39.750
brand value and revenue growth

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:42.503
because frankly it can be
easy to get overexcited

00:07:42.503 --> 00:07:44.797
about the possibility of revenue growth,

00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:46.591
increased distribution,

00:07:46.591 --> 00:07:48.926
and of course, Gucci
have had their own issues

00:07:48.926 --> 00:07:53.431
and challenges on losing
control of the brand experience

00:07:53.431 --> 00:07:55.266
in and out of their history.

00:07:55.266 --> 00:07:57.602
There have been times when
they have over-expanded,

00:07:57.602 --> 00:08:00.438
they lost control of product quality

00:08:00.438 --> 00:08:05.234
in their drive for international success.

00:08:05.234 --> 00:08:08.112
What is it that makes the brand valuable?

00:08:08.112 --> 00:08:10.406
It will often be exclusivity.

00:08:10.406 --> 00:08:12.783
It'll be the control of
the customer experience

00:08:12.783 --> 00:08:14.827
that people can feel confident

00:08:14.827 --> 00:08:16.829
about what they're gonna
get in product terms,

00:08:16.829 --> 00:08:19.081
what they're gonna get in service terms,

00:08:19.081 --> 00:08:21.918
and also the authenticity
that they're going to get

00:08:21.918 --> 00:08:24.045
is part of what they're buying.

00:08:24.045 --> 00:08:25.588
And so therefore you need to make sure

00:08:25.588 --> 00:08:27.798
you are feeding that brand value

00:08:27.798 --> 00:08:30.301
or what it is that
makes the brand valuable

00:08:30.301 --> 00:08:31.594
in the first place.

00:08:33.137 --> 00:08:34.722
So Maurizio had a problem

00:08:34.722 --> 00:08:37.975
because he had a clear vision for Gucci

00:08:37.975 --> 00:08:41.646
but he didn't have the money
to buy out his relatives.

00:08:41.646 --> 00:08:44.857
The answer to his problem
was an investment banker

00:08:44.857 --> 00:08:46.692
who worked for Morgan Stanley at the time

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:47.902
named Andrea Morante.

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:56.369
I can remember very vividly
as if it were yesterday

00:08:56.369 --> 00:09:01.040
that when I explained to the
other partners in the room

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:02.959
the Morgan Stanley room

00:09:02.959 --> 00:09:05.795
that I had met with Maurizio Gucci

00:09:05.795 --> 00:09:07.880
and that he wanted to do

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:11.092
some form of shareholding restructuring,

00:09:11.092 --> 00:09:13.511
I'll never forget that for the first time

00:09:13.511 --> 00:09:16.264
when I was describing a transaction

00:09:16.264 --> 00:09:17.723
everybody was interested.

00:09:17.723 --> 00:09:19.100
And that's, if you will,

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:22.144
the first time that I understood

00:09:22.144 --> 00:09:23.938
the power of a brand.

00:09:25.856 --> 00:09:28.859
So he launches this plan
at a stealth campaign

00:09:28.859 --> 00:09:30.820
with Morgan Stanley to buy up the shares

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:33.155
of Aldo and his cousins,

00:09:33.155 --> 00:09:36.659
and bring in an investment
bank, Investcorp

00:09:36.659 --> 00:09:38.160
as his financial partner.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:39.370
'Cause he didn't have enough money

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:41.289
to just buy it all himself.

00:09:42.331 --> 00:09:45.876
As far as the complexity of purchasing

00:09:45.876 --> 00:09:49.046
the cousins and also the uncle,

00:09:49.046 --> 00:09:52.883
well, that complexity had one solution

00:09:52.883 --> 00:09:56.846
which you could call
today inside information.

00:09:56.846 --> 00:09:59.432
Because I spent a lot
of time with Maurizio,

00:09:59.432 --> 00:10:02.685
he gave me all the inside information

00:10:02.685 --> 00:10:05.146
of what everybody within the family,

00:10:05.146 --> 00:10:07.607
really what the agenda was.

00:10:07.607 --> 00:10:10.651
And in particular, he
also explained to me how

00:10:10.651 --> 00:10:15.281
Paolo Gucci had been
ill-treated by his father

00:10:15.281 --> 00:10:20.244
and given a significant
reduced number of shares

00:10:20.911 --> 00:10:22.246
vis-a-vis his brothers

00:10:22.246 --> 00:10:27.251
because Paolo had literally
took his father to jail.

00:10:29.754 --> 00:10:33.090
So what I did is I proposed to him

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:37.053
to be paid a very similar amount of money

00:10:37.053 --> 00:10:42.016
as if he had not been
penalized by his father.

00:10:42.016 --> 00:10:44.268
I'll pay you the same price

00:10:44.268 --> 00:10:47.146
that your brothers are
going to be receiving.

00:10:48.439 --> 00:10:49.982
He accepted.

00:10:49.982 --> 00:10:54.862
So I actually went there
with a suitcase of cash

00:10:54.862 --> 00:10:58.949
and purchased this initial
3% from Paolo Gucci

00:10:58.949 --> 00:11:03.204
which actually changed the life of Gucci.

00:11:03.204 --> 00:11:05.039
[Interviewer] One person
who worked for the company

00:11:05.039 --> 00:11:08.125
said Paolo has been a traitor to everyone.

00:11:09.919 --> 00:11:10.795
Yes. Maybe.

00:11:10.795 --> 00:11:12.171
Yes.

00:11:12.171 --> 00:11:13.255
Again, these bandits,

00:11:13.255 --> 00:11:16.008
I mean, if you leave them
then you're a traitor, right?

00:11:16.008 --> 00:11:18.636
Once they saw that the game was over

00:11:18.636 --> 00:11:20.096
and there was somebody else coming

00:11:20.096 --> 00:11:22.098
to actually run the business,

00:11:22.098 --> 00:11:27.103
then gradually one by one,
they all sold their shares.

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:31.232
So what initially was
almost a mission impossible

00:11:31.232 --> 00:11:33.025
turned into a reality.

00:11:33.025 --> 00:11:36.362
50% of all the available shares

00:11:36.362 --> 00:11:39.323
were ultimately purchased by Investcorp,

00:11:39.323 --> 00:11:43.786
and at that stage, Investcorp
and Maurizio had the company.

00:11:48.749 --> 00:11:52.294
So Maurizio emerges as
the CEO, he had control

00:11:52.294 --> 00:11:55.631
because even though he
was in a 50/50 agreement

00:11:55.631 --> 00:11:59.051
with Investcorp, they gave
him management control.

00:11:59.051 --> 00:12:02.179
His vision was to take
Gucci back up market

00:12:02.179 --> 00:12:04.849
and that meant quality products,

00:12:04.849 --> 00:12:07.059
beautiful workmanship.

00:12:07.059 --> 00:12:08.853
And he had a very classic idea

00:12:08.853 --> 00:12:10.604
of what he wanted Gucci to be.

00:12:10.604 --> 00:12:13.983
He used to say he wanted
to be round and brown

00:12:13.983 --> 00:12:16.318
and soft to a woman's hand.

00:12:16.318 --> 00:12:20.156
He opened this fabulous
headquarters in Milan,

00:12:20.156 --> 00:12:21.991
no expense spared,

00:12:21.991 --> 00:12:23.784
priceless antiques,

00:12:23.784 --> 00:12:24.660
workmanship.

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:28.831
So the debt started racking
up really, really fast

00:12:28.831 --> 00:12:31.083
and at the same time he had cut out

00:12:31.083 --> 00:12:33.085
the most lucrative parts of the business.

00:12:33.085 --> 00:12:35.671
So, the Gucci accessories collection

00:12:35.671 --> 00:12:38.174
which was the cash cow for the company.

00:12:40.468 --> 00:12:43.596
Things were done in a
somewhat irrational way.

00:12:43.596 --> 00:12:47.308
I think that he incurred
massive costs in Italy

00:12:47.308 --> 00:12:49.769
which the company could
not afford at the time.

00:12:49.769 --> 00:12:52.688
He decided we had to
clean an old new product.

00:12:52.688 --> 00:12:54.231
Okay, well, that's a great idea

00:12:54.231 --> 00:12:56.358
but what is the cost of it?

00:12:56.358 --> 00:12:58.527
So overnight he said, no more GG.

00:12:58.527 --> 00:13:02.198
So I said, "Well, Maurizio,
GG is like 70% of the sales."

00:13:02.198 --> 00:13:03.866
But he wanted to do it all costs

00:13:03.866 --> 00:13:07.286
so the company really went to tailspin

00:13:07.286 --> 00:13:09.747
and started really losing money,

00:13:09.747 --> 00:13:11.957
and was very, very difficult.

00:13:11.957 --> 00:13:13.834
Became very painful.

00:13:17.004 --> 00:13:20.925
So, the situation became
dire with Gucci's finances.

00:13:20.925 --> 00:13:24.011
They were not able to pay salaries.

00:13:24.011 --> 00:13:26.347
They were not able to pay suppliers.

00:13:26.347 --> 00:13:30.184
They were on the verge of having
to take the company's books

00:13:30.184 --> 00:13:32.561
into court to say they
couldn't settle the accounts.

00:13:32.561 --> 00:13:33.604
And at that point,

00:13:33.604 --> 00:13:35.773
that means that the company
has declared bankruptcy.

00:13:35.773 --> 00:13:39.652
So, Gucci is really teetering
on the brink at this stage.

00:13:40.736 --> 00:13:42.571
He had to right these things.

00:13:42.571 --> 00:13:45.491
I think that the problem
he couldn't make it happen.

00:13:45.491 --> 00:13:46.867
There's some people have great vision

00:13:46.867 --> 00:13:49.411
but he had the greatest
vision, the great strategy

00:13:49.411 --> 00:13:50.913
but if you execute he was

00:13:51.914 --> 00:13:53.958
beyond incapable of executing.

00:13:53.958 --> 00:13:55.584
That really was the bottom line.

00:13:56.627 --> 00:13:59.964
So Investcorp after
giving Maurizio free reign

00:13:59.964 --> 00:14:02.466
to put his vision into place,

00:14:02.466 --> 00:14:05.803
they're becoming very, very
worried about the situation,

00:14:05.803 --> 00:14:09.181
and they are millions
of dollars in the hole.

00:14:09.181 --> 00:14:12.101
And they're deciding they don't
wanna put any more money in

00:14:12.101 --> 00:14:14.562
because they think it's
just going down the drain.

00:14:17.231 --> 00:14:20.192
I told him that it was
in his best interest

00:14:20.192 --> 00:14:22.111
in order to keep and preserve

00:14:22.111 --> 00:14:25.781
the good relationship he
had had with Investcorp

00:14:25.781 --> 00:14:27.199
to go to Investcorp and say

00:14:27.199 --> 00:14:31.704
let's choose a common new
chief executive officer,

00:14:31.704 --> 00:14:34.290
somebody who's really
gonna operate the business.

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:36.417
And so, when I told him that

00:14:36.417 --> 00:14:39.336
unfortunately the reaction
was extremely negative

00:14:39.336 --> 00:14:44.174
because for him, I was telling
him, which is the truth,

00:14:44.174 --> 00:14:47.887
that he was not capable
to run the company.

00:14:47.887 --> 00:14:51.056
And I felt the responsibility
of telling him that

00:14:51.056 --> 00:14:51.974
in his own interest

00:14:51.974 --> 00:14:54.143
but sometimes your own interest

00:14:54.143 --> 00:14:58.480
is perceived in a different way.

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:00.816
He is begging them for time.

00:15:00.816 --> 00:15:03.277
His assessment is that the turnaround

00:15:03.277 --> 00:15:05.863
needs a little more time for the customers

00:15:05.863 --> 00:15:08.908
to really get the feeling
for how Gucci's changed.

00:15:08.908 --> 00:15:10.284
And at one point he's begging them,

00:15:10.284 --> 00:15:12.328
"If you just give me six months," he says,

00:15:12.328 --> 00:15:15.497
"the Japanese market is
going to realize that

00:15:15.497 --> 00:15:16.832
there's a new Gucci

00:15:16.832 --> 00:15:19.168
and the wind will be back in our sales."

00:15:19.168 --> 00:15:20.920
Well, he never got that chance.

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:23.797
Investcorp created a war room.

00:15:23.797 --> 00:15:26.342
They went after him with
everything they could

00:15:26.342 --> 00:15:29.637
and finally they were
successful in kicking him out,

00:15:29.637 --> 00:15:34.433
and they bought his 50% in
the company for $150 million

00:15:34.433 --> 00:15:36.477
and then took it over entirely.

00:15:37.436 --> 00:15:40.022
The irony of this story was that actually

00:15:40.022 --> 00:15:41.774
Maurizio was right.

00:15:41.774 --> 00:15:44.401
Six months after he was
forced out by Investcorp,

00:15:45.361 --> 00:15:47.821
the Japanese woke up to the new Gucci

00:15:47.821 --> 00:15:50.741
and started buying Gucci bags,

00:15:50.741 --> 00:15:53.202
and the company started making money,

00:15:53.202 --> 00:15:55.996
hand over fist in a very quick time.

00:16:03.587 --> 00:16:06.924
In March of 1995, one
morning, broad daylight

00:16:06.924 --> 00:16:10.678
Maurizio is walking into the
front door of his new offices,

00:16:10.678 --> 00:16:13.722
a gunman comes into the doorway behind him

00:16:13.722 --> 00:16:15.975
and shoots him dead in cold blood.

00:16:33.367 --> 00:16:35.160
The murder of Maurizio Gucci

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:37.371
was a shocking event for the city

00:16:37.371 --> 00:16:40.082
and in addition to being shocking,

00:16:40.082 --> 00:16:45.004
it was very sinister because
there was no clear culprit.

00:16:45.004 --> 00:16:47.214
The prosecutor who was
investigating the case

00:16:47.214 --> 00:16:49.425
was looking into his business deals,

00:16:49.425 --> 00:16:51.593
he was looking into the family conflicts.

00:16:51.593 --> 00:16:55.139
He interviewed the cousins,
he interviewed Patrizia

00:16:55.139 --> 00:16:59.309
but he didn't really see
a clear family connection.

00:16:59.309 --> 00:17:03.605
And the case was just cold after two years

00:17:03.605 --> 00:17:06.191
until because of a tipster one day,

00:17:06.191 --> 00:17:08.068
one morning at 5:30 in the morning

00:17:08.068 --> 00:17:12.364
the police raid Patrizia's
apartment on Corso Venezia

00:17:12.364 --> 00:17:14.867
and arrest her for Maurizio's murder.

00:17:16.493 --> 00:17:17.911
She had an obsession.

00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:21.623
She wanted her husband dead

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:25.669
and she spoke about it to everyone.

00:17:39.391 --> 00:17:42.770
Often people are remembered
for the wrong reasons

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:47.274
and the glamor and maybe the interest

00:17:47.274 --> 00:17:51.945
has more to do with the homicide.

00:17:51.945 --> 00:17:55.657
A wife appoints a killer, a hitman

00:17:55.657 --> 00:17:57.534
and perhaps there is a risk

00:17:57.534 --> 00:18:01.497
that Maurizio will indeed
be remembered as somebody

00:18:01.497 --> 00:18:04.625
who was killed by his
wife for no reason at all,

00:18:04.625 --> 00:18:09.129
or for very good reasons
depending on where you sit.

00:18:09.129 --> 00:18:12.049
But I think that Maurizio's real legacy

00:18:12.049 --> 00:18:15.594
is that the Gucci brand
has really been created

00:18:15.594 --> 00:18:17.763
by three generations.

00:18:17.763 --> 00:18:22.351
As often as the case in
some family businesses,

00:18:22.351 --> 00:18:24.812
after the third generation,

00:18:24.812 --> 00:18:28.524
the family is not able to
keep control of the company.

00:18:28.524 --> 00:18:32.486
In this case, Maurizio not only
lost control of the company

00:18:32.486 --> 00:18:33.779
but he also lost his life.

00:18:44.123 --> 00:18:46.375
In the early stages of many luxury brands,

00:18:46.375 --> 00:18:51.380
the founder, the visionary,
the original creative director,

00:18:51.463 --> 00:18:53.799
they came almost to the
end of their tenure,

00:18:53.799 --> 00:18:57.094
and they needed somebody
else to take the brand on

00:18:57.094 --> 00:18:59.680
in a different direction
or a fresh direction.

00:18:59.680 --> 00:19:02.141
I think you could also
say, with the Gucci brand,

00:19:02.141 --> 00:19:04.268
that's a hundred years old,

00:19:04.268 --> 00:19:08.063
over time, a brand like that
needs to have fresh input,

00:19:08.063 --> 00:19:09.523
and fresh eyes.

00:19:13.485 --> 00:19:15.779
So even though Maurizio wasn't ultimately

00:19:15.779 --> 00:19:18.949
successful in putting his vision in place,

00:19:18.949 --> 00:19:20.993
he had actually done a lot of the work

00:19:20.993 --> 00:19:23.829
to set Gucci up for it's next phase.

00:19:25.038 --> 00:19:28.458
He hired an American retail
executive, Dawn Mello,

00:19:28.458 --> 00:19:31.587
who was the president of
Bergdorf Goodman, at the time,

00:19:31.587 --> 00:19:35.966
and one of her main contributions
was to hire, Tom Ford.

00:19:39.261 --> 00:19:40.679
After Maurizio left,

00:19:40.679 --> 00:19:43.849
they started giving me more
and more responsibility,

00:19:43.849 --> 00:19:46.518
and I became the Chief Operating Officer

00:19:46.518 --> 00:19:48.228
of the company very quickly.

00:19:48.228 --> 00:19:50.564
And at that point, I really,

00:19:50.564 --> 00:19:52.774
de facto, I was running a company,

00:19:52.774 --> 00:19:54.693
they basically asked me who I thought to

00:19:54.693 --> 00:19:56.403
be the Creative Director,

00:19:56.403 --> 00:19:59.489
and I felt very strongly to be Tom.

00:19:59.489 --> 00:20:03.035
I remember very vividly,
I drove up to Milan,

00:20:03.035 --> 00:20:04.661
and Tom was very, very young.

00:20:04.661 --> 00:20:06.747
We had a meeting and I said Tom,

00:20:06.747 --> 00:20:09.374
I think we can really fix this company.

00:20:09.374 --> 00:20:10.167
So, I said look,

00:20:10.167 --> 00:20:12.794
I'm not interested in
interfering, I'm a businessman.

00:20:12.794 --> 00:20:16.215
I don't pretend to be a
creative person by any means,

00:20:16.215 --> 00:20:18.717
so you're in charge, you know, you do it,

00:20:18.717 --> 00:20:21.428
and let's see where we go here.

00:20:21.428 --> 00:20:24.890
He accepted and that's really
the beginning of the story.

00:20:27.517 --> 00:20:28.602
He bought back the logo,

00:20:28.602 --> 00:20:30.062
and make it chic.

00:20:30.062 --> 00:20:31.939
He makes clothes that rock the world,

00:20:31.939 --> 00:20:34.816
and he put sex back where it belongs.

00:20:34.816 --> 00:20:37.236
Here's Tom Ford for Gucci.

00:20:37.236 --> 00:20:39.446
Thank you. I'd like to thank Vogue.

00:20:39.446 --> 00:20:41.073
Tom, you're the greatest!

00:20:41.073 --> 00:20:42.741
Thank You!

00:20:42.741 --> 00:20:44.201
Thank you.

00:20:44.201 --> 00:20:48.038
It's difficult to understate the impact of

00:20:48.038 --> 00:20:49.998
Tom Ford on Gucci.

00:20:49.998 --> 00:20:51.291
No, it's not just about fashion,

00:20:51.291 --> 00:20:53.168
has purchased 5% of the company.

00:20:53.168 --> 00:20:53.710
it's also about real life.

00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:57.422
With his extraordinary style, innovation,

00:20:57.422 --> 00:20:59.841
and actually his sense of control,

00:20:59.841 --> 00:21:03.929
this is one of the really
really key success criteria.

00:21:03.929 --> 00:21:06.265
There has to be a vision,

00:21:06.265 --> 00:21:10.435
and it has to be a very direct vision.

00:21:10.435 --> 00:21:12.437
Tom Ford has a very, very clear,

00:21:12.437 --> 00:21:15.732
and strong idea about what Gucci could be,

00:21:15.732 --> 00:21:17.693
in terms of global ambition.

00:21:17.693 --> 00:21:19.945
He made sure that his vision

00:21:19.945 --> 00:21:23.198
was totally controlled right
across the customer experience.

00:21:23.198 --> 00:21:25.993
From the advertising, every
point of communication,

00:21:25.993 --> 00:21:27.828
product detail, and so on.

00:21:27.828 --> 00:21:30.330
Ford's design power hit the fashion world

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:31.581
like a tidal wave.

00:21:31.581 --> 00:21:32.416
The winner is,

00:21:32.416 --> 00:21:33.625
And the winner is

00:21:33.625 --> 00:21:35.335
Tom Ford.

00:21:35.335 --> 00:21:36.211
Thank you, thanks.

00:21:40.299 --> 00:21:43.176
So, the "Tom-Dom" era of Gucci

00:21:43.176 --> 00:21:45.679
really was extraordinarily exciting,

00:21:45.679 --> 00:21:49.474
and it was clear from the
very first menswear show,

00:21:49.474 --> 00:21:52.102
actually, that Tom designed in Florence,

00:21:52.102 --> 00:21:54.438
that something had changed at Gucci,

00:21:54.438 --> 00:21:56.606
and there was a new Gucci in the air.

00:21:56.606 --> 00:21:58.900
And sales took off immediately,

00:21:58.900 --> 00:22:00.986
and even though they
were showing, kind of,

00:22:00.986 --> 00:22:03.238
more sexy styles on the runway,

00:22:03.238 --> 00:22:06.199
they were still selling primarily
black and brown handbags,

00:22:06.199 --> 00:22:08.660
but they really had lit
a fire under this brand,

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:10.245
and the sales are doubling.

00:22:12.039 --> 00:22:13.915
So the sales went through the roof.

00:22:13.915 --> 00:22:16.960
In 1995, our sales doubled.

00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:19.421
The success was just unbelievable.

00:22:19.421 --> 00:22:21.465
Suddenly, really things looked up,

00:22:21.465 --> 00:22:24.885
and then at that point
Investcorp wanted to go public

00:22:24.885 --> 00:22:26.219
as fast as possible.

00:22:28.638 --> 00:22:31.892
So, the Gucci IPO was very anticipated,

00:22:31.892 --> 00:22:34.478
but, what they realized was that,

00:22:34.478 --> 00:22:37.606
there was no fashion sector
in the financial market,

00:22:37.606 --> 00:22:40.442
so they actually had to train analysts in

00:22:40.442 --> 00:22:42.861
how the luxury market worked, and so,

00:22:42.861 --> 00:22:47.199
Domenico's being given
presentation workshops, and skills.

00:22:47.199 --> 00:22:49.284
They are bringing analysts into the room,

00:22:49.284 --> 00:22:51.661
and talking about how the industry works.

00:22:51.661 --> 00:22:55.123
They really pioneered the luxury sector

00:22:55.123 --> 00:22:57.292
in the financial market.

00:22:57.292 --> 00:23:00.003
And other brands subsequently followed.

00:23:00.003 --> 00:23:03.465
But, it was really the Gucci
IPO that set the stage.

00:23:03.465 --> 00:23:05.926
The old Gucci name sure is attracting

00:23:05.926 --> 00:23:09.304
a lot of customers these
days, on Main St. and Wall St.

00:23:09.304 --> 00:23:12.432
The IPO of Gucci was even more successful

00:23:12.432 --> 00:23:13.934
than their wildest dreams.

00:23:13.934 --> 00:23:16.019
They debuted at 22 dollars,

00:23:16.019 --> 00:23:18.980
and shot immediately up to 26 dollars.

00:23:18.980 --> 00:23:22.317
The offering was 14 times over subscribed,

00:23:22.317 --> 00:23:25.946
and ended up making more
than 2 billion dollars

00:23:25.946 --> 00:23:27.280
for Investcorp.

00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:31.034
Sales were up 113%, so we were delighted,

00:23:31.034 --> 00:23:32.744
it was the fifth quarter in a row

00:23:32.744 --> 00:23:34.704
with which we double our sale.

00:23:34.704 --> 00:23:37.499
It proved to be a success story,

00:23:37.499 --> 00:23:41.711
certainly from a financial
perspective, for Investcorp.

00:23:41.711 --> 00:23:45.507
But also a huge success
story, within the industry.

00:23:46.466 --> 00:23:51.054
Once it was clear that the luxury markets

00:23:51.054 --> 00:23:54.307
was there to be developed,

00:23:54.307 --> 00:23:57.769
and was there to be taken advantage of,

00:23:57.769 --> 00:24:02.774
all the players who were
already in that market

00:24:02.816 --> 00:24:05.569
had the competitive advantage

00:24:05.569 --> 00:24:07.696
of having seen it from within.

00:24:09.114 --> 00:24:11.324
The risk was that somebody
could take it over,

00:24:11.324 --> 00:24:13.743
and given this massive success.

00:24:13.743 --> 00:24:15.996
And it doesn't matter
how successful we are,

00:24:15.996 --> 00:24:16.913
we're a public company.

00:24:16.913 --> 00:24:20.500
There is always moment, in
which the stock may go down.

00:24:20.500 --> 00:24:22.627
In 1997, Gucci was a

00:24:22.627 --> 00:24:24.379
high performance machine,

00:24:24.379 --> 00:24:27.174
but a financial crisis
hit Asia, putting Gucci,

00:24:27.174 --> 00:24:30.510
and the rest of the luxury
goods market in peril.

00:24:31.595 --> 00:24:33.388
Japan was the dominant force

00:24:33.388 --> 00:24:35.932
in the luxury market at the time.

00:24:35.932 --> 00:24:39.060
So, when there was this crisis in Asia,

00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:40.937
the stock went down.

00:24:40.937 --> 00:24:43.940
And I think at that point was LVMH

00:24:43.940 --> 00:24:46.902
started purchasing shares of the company.

00:24:46.902 --> 00:24:49.446
That really was my concern
about the possibility

00:24:49.446 --> 00:24:51.740
of it being open to a takeover.

00:24:56.077 --> 00:24:58.038
So, the real sort of rival on the

00:24:58.038 --> 00:25:02.250
market for Gucci was
LVMH and Bernard Arnault,

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:05.212
who was kind of known
as a corporate raider

00:25:05.212 --> 00:25:07.380
in the European luxury scene.

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:11.051
He had taken over Louis
Vuitton, Moet Hennessy,

00:25:11.051 --> 00:25:13.803
and had other fashion brands, he had Dior.

00:25:14.763 --> 00:25:18.433
We tried to build a large business

00:25:18.433 --> 00:25:23.396
with our partners with one
criteria, the best quality,

00:25:23.688 --> 00:25:27.442
and the most elitist product,

00:25:27.442 --> 00:25:30.987
in every line that we are
selling throughout the world.

00:25:33.365 --> 00:25:35.617
I got a call from Yves Carcelle,

00:25:35.617 --> 00:25:38.495
who is the CEO of Louis Vuitton,

00:25:38.495 --> 00:25:41.623
and he called me, told me that LVMH

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:46.795
And at that time if you
went above the 5% threshold,

00:25:46.795 --> 00:25:48.964
under the rule of the
New York Stock Exchange,

00:25:48.964 --> 00:25:50.549
you had to go public with it.

00:25:50.549 --> 00:25:53.343
So, that was really the
beginning of the battle

00:25:53.343 --> 00:25:54.678
with LVMH.

00:25:54.678 --> 00:25:56.012
And didn't he say something about

00:25:56.012 --> 00:25:57.764
how it was a friendly acquisition?

00:25:57.764 --> 00:26:00.016
Yeah, he say it was very very friendly.

00:26:00.016 --> 00:26:03.144
He say, well you know
Arnault, Mr. Arnault,

00:26:03.144 --> 00:26:04.646
has a hell of a reputation,

00:26:04.646 --> 00:26:07.732
so I'm not sure how
friendly this is gonna be.

00:26:07.732 --> 00:26:10.402
Now were gonna mix a little
money with a lot of style.

00:26:10.402 --> 00:26:13.154
From January through
June, two retail titans

00:26:13.154 --> 00:26:16.366
battle for control of
fashion's biggest prize,

00:26:16.366 --> 00:26:18.660
the House of Gucci.

00:26:18.660 --> 00:26:20.996
So, once Arnault had thrown down

00:26:20.996 --> 00:26:23.832
the gauntlet for Gucci,
Domenico De Sole realized

00:26:23.832 --> 00:26:25.959
that he was in for a big fight,

00:26:25.959 --> 00:26:29.546
and he pulled in bankers and advisors,

00:26:29.546 --> 00:26:31.840
and decided to stage a resistance,

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:35.260
and his main concern was
that he didn't want Arnault

00:26:35.260 --> 00:26:38.096
to be able to take over
control of the company

00:26:38.096 --> 00:26:43.059
without offering a full and
fair offer to all shareholders.

00:26:43.893 --> 00:26:46.646
They are our number one competitor.

00:26:46.646 --> 00:26:50.150
We compete against them for customers.

00:26:50.150 --> 00:26:52.402
We compete for talented people.

00:26:52.402 --> 00:26:54.362
This is a case of creeping control.

00:26:54.362 --> 00:26:56.239
They would like to control the company

00:26:56.239 --> 00:26:58.450
without paying the full price.

00:26:58.450 --> 00:27:00.952
The contest between these two companies

00:27:00.952 --> 00:27:02.871
was getting more and more acrimonious,

00:27:02.871 --> 00:27:05.457
and with the help of his advisors,

00:27:05.457 --> 00:27:08.585
De Sole put protection
mechanism into place

00:27:08.585 --> 00:27:11.379
through the issuing of employee shares,

00:27:11.379 --> 00:27:14.758
and that kept on diluting Arnault's stake,

00:27:14.758 --> 00:27:17.552
as he's building up his
stake in the company.

00:27:17.552 --> 00:27:20.055
But at at certain point, De Sole realizes,

00:27:20.055 --> 00:27:21.348
that they aren't going to be able to

00:27:21.348 --> 00:27:23.767
fend off Arnault forever.

00:27:23.767 --> 00:27:27.562
So, kind of in the ninth
hour, Francois Pinault,

00:27:27.562 --> 00:27:31.232
who runs PPR, which was
a French retail group,

00:27:31.232 --> 00:27:34.694
comes in and offers to
buy a stake in Gucci,

00:27:34.694 --> 00:27:38.198
and becomes the white knight
to help them fight off Arnault.

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:42.744
Francois was the most decisive man

00:27:42.744 --> 00:27:44.704
in the history of the universe.

00:27:44.704 --> 00:27:46.039
I talked to him for half an hour,

00:27:46.039 --> 00:27:47.582
I told my story and I explained

00:27:47.582 --> 00:27:52.170
why we wanted to defend against Arnault.

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:54.047
De Sole drove a hard bargain,

00:27:54.047 --> 00:27:57.217
securing 3 billion for
Gucci's immediate growth.

00:27:57.217 --> 00:28:00.011
It was the very deal
Bernard Arnault rejected.

00:28:01.471 --> 00:28:03.390
So Francois Pinault does not only

00:28:03.390 --> 00:28:06.935
want to be the white knight,
but he has an even bigger idea,

00:28:06.935 --> 00:28:10.647
and so, he turns Yves Saint
Laurent, 3 billion dollars

00:28:10.647 --> 00:28:12.357
over to Tom and Dom, and he says,

00:28:12.357 --> 00:28:15.694
I want you, kind of, the
dream team of fashion,

00:28:15.694 --> 00:28:17.404
to buy up other brands,

00:28:17.404 --> 00:28:19.989
and turn Gucci into a multi brand group,

00:28:19.989 --> 00:28:23.535
that can rival Arnault and LVMH.

00:28:23.535 --> 00:28:25.453
That was pretty brutal because,

00:28:25.453 --> 00:28:27.664
we were under pressure to do it, okay.

00:28:27.664 --> 00:28:29.416
So it was just a lot of work.

00:28:29.416 --> 00:28:31.418
There were a lot of potential targets,

00:28:31.418 --> 00:28:33.002
and every time we tried to buy something,

00:28:33.002 --> 00:28:35.755
all of a sudden, LVMH would show up.

00:28:35.755 --> 00:28:37.507
It was not an easy thing to do,

00:28:37.507 --> 00:28:40.176
so everything had to be
done in great secrecy.

00:28:40.176 --> 00:28:41.678
It was a huge amount of pressure,

00:28:41.678 --> 00:28:45.223
because it was on one
end running the company,

00:28:45.223 --> 00:28:47.767
and on the other end
acquiring all these companies,

00:28:47.767 --> 00:28:50.437
and we acquired a lot
of companies very fast.

00:28:55.942 --> 00:28:58.361
To not only turn around Gucci,

00:28:58.361 --> 00:29:01.281
or keep the Gucci turn around going,

00:29:01.281 --> 00:29:03.533
but to also then identify other talent,

00:29:03.533 --> 00:29:06.536
other brands, make them successful,

00:29:06.536 --> 00:29:08.747
I mean, it became a huge endeavor.

00:29:11.124 --> 00:29:13.835
They were both working 24/7.

00:29:13.835 --> 00:29:17.422
Tom was designing both Gucci
and Yves Saint Laurent,

00:29:17.422 --> 00:29:19.924
and at the same time, they
were trying to identify,

00:29:19.924 --> 00:29:22.844
and then nurture these other designers.

00:29:22.844 --> 00:29:24.262
I was looking for major investment,

00:29:24.262 --> 00:29:28.933
and then Gucci got in touch
and the rest is history.

00:29:28.933 --> 00:29:30.769
They bought Alexander McQueen.

00:29:30.769 --> 00:29:32.103
They bought Stella McCartney.

00:29:32.103 --> 00:29:34.022
They bought Balenciaga.

00:29:34.022 --> 00:29:35.565
Each brand was slightly different,

00:29:35.565 --> 00:29:37.609
you know, some were young
designers that needed

00:29:37.609 --> 00:29:39.903
sort of, the help to grow and to mature.

00:29:39.903 --> 00:29:43.782
Others were faded names that
needed to be turned around.

00:29:43.782 --> 00:29:46.868
So they had a lot of children

00:29:46.868 --> 00:29:50.079
that they were trying to
raise, in this endeavor.

00:29:51.831 --> 00:29:54.083
There was the agreement that we would

00:29:54.083 --> 00:29:56.461
run the company independently for 5 years,

00:29:56.461 --> 00:29:58.797
create this multi brand company,

00:29:58.797 --> 00:30:00.924
this large group of companies,

00:30:00.924 --> 00:30:03.927
and then he would buy 100% of the share,

00:30:03.927 --> 00:30:05.845
at the price that had been set forth,

00:30:05.845 --> 00:30:08.807
at the time of the battle with LVMH.

00:30:10.433 --> 00:30:14.562
So, five years after the
initial white knight, Pinault,

00:30:14.562 --> 00:30:17.023
buys the full 100% of Gucci,

00:30:17.023 --> 00:30:20.318
and, you know, Tom and
Dom are in a situation,

00:30:20.318 --> 00:30:23.112
where they are essentially running this

00:30:23.112 --> 00:30:25.865
publicly traded company as
though it were their own.

00:30:25.865 --> 00:30:29.077
Until a time comes where
Pinault is taking control,

00:30:29.077 --> 00:30:32.455
and it's time to renegotiate the contracts

00:30:32.455 --> 00:30:34.207
for Tom and Domenico,

00:30:34.207 --> 00:30:36.751
and, they realized that
they aren't going to

00:30:36.751 --> 00:30:39.796
have the freedom to run
Gucci, they way they had been,

00:30:39.796 --> 00:30:42.340
and Tom and Dom announced

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:44.342
that they were resigning from Gucci.

00:30:44.342 --> 00:30:47.178
Which is kind of a shock to
the industry, at that time.

00:30:55.061 --> 00:30:58.106
So at the end, Tom was
clear, he was not gonna stay,

00:30:58.106 --> 00:31:00.483
and I worked with him
so closely, you know,

00:31:00.483 --> 00:31:02.986
was just like my younger brother,

00:31:02.986 --> 00:31:05.655
so at the end, we felt, and I felt,

00:31:05.655 --> 00:31:07.699
that we had done our part,

00:31:07.699 --> 00:31:11.077
and it was time to move on with life.

00:31:12.871 --> 00:31:14.956
The partnership between Tom and Domenico

00:31:14.956 --> 00:31:16.749
really became foundational.

00:31:16.749 --> 00:31:18.668
Not only for the Gucci business,

00:31:18.668 --> 00:31:21.129
but for other luxury houses,

00:31:21.129 --> 00:31:22.630
that were starting to replicate

00:31:22.630 --> 00:31:26.676
the synergy between the
creative and the management.

00:31:26.676 --> 00:31:30.430
It became seen as kind of
the essential component

00:31:30.430 --> 00:31:32.390
that you needed in that period to run

00:31:32.390 --> 00:31:34.183
a successful luxury brand.

00:31:35.059 --> 00:31:38.354
Gucci is my legacy, so I love the company,

00:31:38.354 --> 00:31:42.108
and I'm super happy about Gucci.

00:31:42.108 --> 00:31:45.194
Some people ask me this question, a lot.

00:31:45.194 --> 00:31:47.447
No, I always wanted Gucci to do well.

00:31:47.447 --> 00:31:49.449
You know, I think it would
have been catastrophic

00:31:49.449 --> 00:31:52.076
if Gucci didn't do well
after Tom and I left.

00:31:54.037 --> 00:31:56.164
The genius, in many ways, of Gucci,

00:31:56.164 --> 00:31:59.375
is how it's been able to reinvent itself,

00:31:59.375 --> 00:32:03.379
and also frankly, reinvent
the sex of its times.

00:32:04.589 --> 00:32:09.594
If we think about how Gucci
was rescued in the 1990s,

00:32:09.594 --> 00:32:13.723
sex sells and that
contemporary view on sex

00:32:13.723 --> 00:32:16.351
was a fundamentally
important part of that.

00:32:18.227 --> 00:32:21.689
Now we've seen Gucci
really go ahead again.

00:32:21.689 --> 00:32:24.400
The last few years have been
phenomenally successful.

00:32:24.400 --> 00:32:25.443
Now why is that?

00:32:25.443 --> 00:32:27.111
Well, actually, they've expressed sex

00:32:27.111 --> 00:32:28.863
in a rather different way.

00:32:28.863 --> 00:32:32.408
A gender blending sex, a
non-binary type of sex,

00:32:32.408 --> 00:32:36.371
that has really really
resonated with millennials,

00:32:36.371 --> 00:32:39.582
and consumers under the age of 35,

00:32:39.582 --> 00:32:41.751
and that has been both very unusual,

00:32:41.751 --> 00:32:44.712
it's been very challenging
for luxury brands,

00:32:44.712 --> 00:32:48.466
but, of course, Gucci have
made it a truly runaway,

00:32:48.466 --> 00:32:49.842
and runway success.

